[17592186504586 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dfe9a209-2cb9-5126-b3aa-2aa8747d8994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504590 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504590 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504590 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504590 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504590 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb18eeee-c519-5ea4-9cf3-8aeda17a4734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504595 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f32805e9-8f0e-5643-b8cd-67061494f218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504598 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8975b1d-a5d0-5edc-93b1-10d5e6b649c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{x+3}, & x\\ne -3 \\\\ 2, & x=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28d2bedc-f4b0-58dc-80a4-886e3d716859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504614 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if  $-\\frac{3}{x+3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{3}{x+3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+3=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}(-\\frac{3}{x+3}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48610f07da2103a0e1416318c1c70da426d8c845f0870387836098287d83efb8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6d/6d3cbdc4a0158ebc827c2f991b446f0b9464322f8596b97176af55aa39b4a72d.png)\n\nBased on the graph of $y=f(x)$  shown above, the function $f$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f8dfca9-4caa-51a0-a26a-4299455440e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504622 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=-2$ and $x=0$  but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51bf4893-7d23-5880-bdd4-7c570a118b95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504626 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-3x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-4)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1,4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-1$ and $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104854" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & x\\le 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57542381-13e5-59f8-9ea4-61779c51f267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504631 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $\\frac{1}{x-3}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the vertical asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{x-3}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x-3})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/13/13817b066347391aae800b751c50a7a411dc06372d346e0d9b87e6f3fe3ca1ba.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1071 "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x}$ \nhas discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f60286b-a701-5a91-9733-7afc68b9ba06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504634 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptote, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{3{x}^{2}+5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(3x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptote by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x+5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3},0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-values  where the function  $f(x)$ has discontinuities caused by vertical asymptotes are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3713990-073a-5c59-933e-791d16a2c49e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504659 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x+2}{{x}^{2}-2x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+2)}{(x+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+2)}{\\cancel{(x+1)}(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=3$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73429" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9115fbc-4fac-58df-8796-79802b979ca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504664 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+13x+15}{2{x}^{2}-7x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(2x+3)}{(x-5)(2x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}}{(x-5)\\cancel{(2x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\,⟹\\,x=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=5$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adf0b53d-c3e9-5c25-81fa-45a5c95eb4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504666 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+20}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)(x-4)}{(x-4)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-5)\\cancel{(x-4)}}{\\cancel{(x-4)}(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-5}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+4=0\\,⟹\\,x=-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{x-1}, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas an  infinite discontinuity at $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af7b8af8-e50c-5536-8725-7ed61d38a3dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504667 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either ${x}^{2}-1$ or $\\frac{2}{x-1}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of ${x}^{2}-1.$ Since ${x}^{2}-1$ is a polynomial, therefore it cannot grow without bound due to a vertical asymptote. So this function cannot have an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{2}{x-1}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x>1$, we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{x-1})=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the only point where the function $y=f(x)$ has an infinite discontinuity is  $x=1.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cba4d1e7cab2340e24d176cee3e03c6a103cf0c69fbf15209c5f4b92a4993e2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0c/0cfcb2f5e07b52b28cb40ed293996fa3699aab4759093513f7386a4d8c3024c1.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8d4049d-0cbf-5d7d-aff8-ae877201f7a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504671 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=3.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1071 "From **left to right**, the $x$-values  where the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-x}, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8-x}, & x\\ge 5,\\,x\\ne 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n has an infinite discontinuity are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,$ $x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bccf9705-1345-5b91-b48e-5b2d9522eb17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504674 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\frac{1}{5-x}$ or $\\frac{1}{8-x}$ grow without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{5-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n5-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x<5,$ which excludes $x=5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{5-x})=+\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{8-x}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\n8-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $5\\le x<8$ and $x>8,$ which excludes $x=8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=\\infty \\,,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{8-x})=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=8$ is an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the function $y=f(x)$ has infinite discontinuities at \n\n$$\nx=5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7773030c42d9e26a737ad1ed0208900101ed291189c7a1bc1430a808659ba33.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/66/661520d112c7c7ef1c4591c3708f28fdb900d9ae6d036f774593239318205e09.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c798fdda-953e-5d91-a2f9-32a46093ebbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504678 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c899081f-aaf3-5d73-a009-6354d1a2e255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504679 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(x-5)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdf00b6e-c9c6-53a7-abba-5979171f25e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504683 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5-x}{3{x}^{2}-10x-25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(x-5)}{(3x+5)(x-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\cancel{(x-5)}}{(3x+5)\\cancel{(x-5)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n3x+5=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d79ab0ee-343a-572d-8ea7-20c9b4a3405e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504694 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{x(3x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\nx(3x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2b2de31-2e08-55fe-906d-b2e8d713411d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504701 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}-3x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(2x+1)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x-2)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42266841cb4c8099a7ed7a0a5f5e14bc6f517c2a83bbeee7a02427d2ba44a208.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f32b0e13-9234-5f0f-8ec6-055e7e55ffd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504705 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ \n\nThere are also discontinuities at $x=1$ and $x=3,$ but they are not discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x}$ has a discontinuity caused by a vertical asymptote at\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f55b2c6c-0d34-5876-a0ba-01591a852f5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504707 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x+3}{{x}^{2}+3x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(2x+1)}{x(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+3)}(2x+1)}{x\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{x+8}, & x\\ne -8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdd8bd57-2d2f-5ace-b80e-0473ac5a0a46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504708 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if $-\\frac{1}{x+8}$ grows without bound within its respective subdomain.\n\nWe compute the asymptotes of $-\\frac{1}{x+8}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-8.\n$$\n\nAlthough both branches of the function are defined for $x\\ne -8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-8)^{+}}(-\\frac{1}{x+8}) & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=-8$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed2cae2a50cb15f86ead5831990366db2048c100d71dc9d1b681b890da38844f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
